      ":question/math-academy-id": "e-457",
      ":question/problem": "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{1/2}x(1-2x)^5\\,\\textrm d x.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=1-2x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad -\\dfrac 1 2 \\,\\textrm d u = \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe also note that $x = \\dfrac 1 2 (1-u).$ \n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get\n\n| **$x$** | $0$ | $1/2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **$u$** | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nWe can now solve the integral using our new variable $u,$ remembering to use the new limits of integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}x(1-2x)^{5}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{0}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (1-u)\\cdot {u}^{5}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{0}(1-u)\\cdot {u}^{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{0}({u}^{5}-{u}^{6})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{(\\frac{1}{6}{u}^{6}-\\frac{1}{7}{u}^{7})|}_{1}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}([0-0]-[\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{1}{7}]) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}(-\\frac{1}{42}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{168}\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
