        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-13191",
        ":question/problem": "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-2}^{0}x{(x+2)}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=x+2.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe also note that $x=u-2.$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get\n\n| **$x$** | $-2$ | $0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **$u$** | $0$ | $2$ |\n\nWe can now solve the integral using our new variable $u,$ remembering to use the new limits of integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}x{(x+2)}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(u-2){u}^{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}({u}^{4}-2{u}^{3})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{5}{u}^{5}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{u}^{4})|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{5}{u}^{5}-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{4})|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}({2}^{5}-{0}^{5})-\\frac{1}{2}({2}^{4}-{0}^{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(32-0)-\\frac{1}{2}(16-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{5}-8 \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
