        ":question/id": "7fd825e3-0d14-5ed8-a4b9-9f4979a0fb9f",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-346390",
        ":question/problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-1/2}^{3/2}4x\\sqrt{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=2x+1.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe also note that $x=\\frac{1}{2}(u-1).$ \n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get\n\n| **$x$** | $-1/2$ | $3/2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **$u$** | $0$ | $4$ |\n\nWe can now solve the integral using our new variable $u,$ remembering to use the new limits of integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1/2}^{3/2}4x\\sqrt{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(u-1)\\cdot \\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}({u}^{3/2}-{u}^{1/2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{(5/2)}{u}^{5/2}-\\frac{1}{(3/2)}{u}^{3/2})|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{2}{5}{u}^{5/2}-\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2})|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {4}^{5/2}-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {4}^{3/2})-(\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {0}^{5/2}-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {0}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{5}-\\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{112}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$"
