        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-346393",
        ":question/problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{6}\\frac{3x+1}{\\sqrt{x+3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=x+3.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe also note that $x=u-3.$ \n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get\n\n| **$x$** | $1$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **$u$** | $4$ | $9$ |\n\nWe can now solve the integral using our new variable $u,$ remembering to use the new limits of integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{6}\\frac{3x+1}{\\sqrt{x+3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{4}^{9}\\frac{3(u-3)+1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{9}\\frac{3u-8}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{9}(3{u}^{1/2}-8{u}^{-1/2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{(3/2)}{u}^{3/2}-\\frac{8}{(1/2)}{u}^{1/2})|}_{4}^{9} \\\\  & ={(2{u}^{3/2}-16{u}^{1/2})|}_{4}^{9} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot {9}^{3/2}-16\\cdot {9}^{1/2})-(2\\cdot {4}^{3/2}-16\\cdot {4}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 27-16\\cdot 3)-(2\\cdot 8-16\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =(54-48)-(16-32) \\\\  & =6-(-16) \\\\  & =22\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
