        ":question/problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-3}^{-2}\\frac{48(x+2)}{{({x}^{2}+4x)}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u={x}^{2}+4x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x+4\\,⟹\\,2(x+2)\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get\n\n| **$x$** | $-3$ | $-2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **$u$** | $-3$ | $-4$ |\n\nWe can now solve the integral using our new variable $u,$ remembering to use the new limits of integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{-2}\\frac{48(x+2)}{{({x}^{2}+4x)}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =24{\\int }_{-3}^{-2}\\frac{1}{{({x}^{2}+4x)}^{3}}\\cdot 2(x+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =24{\\int }_{-3}^{-4}\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =24{\\int }_{-3}^{-4}{u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =24\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-2})|}_{-3}^{-4} \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{12}{{u}^{2}})|}_{-3}^{-4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{(-4)^{2}}-(-\\frac{12}{(-3)^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{16}-(-\\frac{12}{9}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
