        ":question/problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{6{x}^{5}}{({x}^{3}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u={x}^{3}+1.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe also note that ${x}^{3}=u-1.$ \n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get\n\n| **$x$** | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **$u$** | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nWe can now solve the integral using our new variable $u,$ remembering to use the new limits of integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{6{x}^{5}}{({x}^{3}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{({x}^{3}+1)^{2}}\\cdot 3{x}^{2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{2(u-1)}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{2}{u}-\\frac{2}{{u}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+\\frac{2}{u})|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\frac{2}{2})-(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+2) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
