        ":question/problem": "Given that $u=2x-1$, which integral is equivalent to ${\\int }_{1}^{3}x\\sqrt{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Since $u=2x-1$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe also note that $x=\\frac{u+1}{2}.$ \n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get\n\n| **$x$** | $1$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **$u$** | $1$ | $5$ |\n\nWe can now solve the integral using our new variable $u,$ remembering to use the new limits of integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}x\\sqrt{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{u+1}{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{5}(u+1){u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{5}({u}^{3/2}+{u}^{1/2})\\text{d}u\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
